<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Named libc/POSIX aggregate types in the prototype tables (`option libctypes
    off|opaque`).

    The built-in prototype tables' type vocabulary is width-stable, so every
    aggregate pointer in them is spelled `void *`: `fopen` returns one, `fclose`
    takes one, `__uflow` takes one. That is honest about the width and silent
    about the pointee, and the pointee is the one thing a declaration actually
    knows.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture (the `option` command
    needs one before the first `load file`). Every pass clears it and loads a
    real ELF.

    Passes 1 and 2 load `libctypes_stat_x86_64` (built from the `.c` beside it),
    which was written to hold the two halves apart:

      * it declares its stream handles `void *` itself and never names `FILE`,
        so nothing in its DWARF defines a stream type and the named table has to
        MINT one  -  `fclose(void *)` in pass 1, `fclose(FILE *)` in pass 2;
      * it hands `stat` a real `struct stat *`, so its DWARF defines `struct stat`
        with its real members. `st->st_mode` must read the same in BOTH passes
        (#5) and no `field_0x` fallback may appear anywhere (#6).

    #5 and #6 are the ones that can fail. A named shell interned BEFORE the DWARF
    importer completes the same name is completed for everybody except the
    pointers already built against it  -  completion re-keys the struct into a new
    object  -  and this function degrades to
    `*(int *)&st->field_0x18`. The pass runs after the DWARF importer and adopts
    what is already there, which is what these two assertions pin.

    Pass 3 loads the real coreutils `fmt`, whose DWARF DOES define the stream
    type, under glibc's own `struct _IO_FILE` spelling. The named table adopts
    that one rather than minting a second stream type beside it (#7, #8), and it
    keeps its real field layout (#9).

    Passes 4 and 5 load `libctypes_glibc_x86_64`, which carries NO debug info, so
    every named aggregate in it is minted by the table rather than adopted  -  the
    only arm in which `option libctypes glibc` installs a field layout at all.
    Pass 4 is `opaque` (the shipped default) and pass 5 is `glibc`, over the same
    two functions: the `getc_unlocked` refill body, whose two stream accesses go
    from `*(uint1 **)&amp;a0-&gt;field_0x8` to `a0-&gt;_IO_read_ptr` (#10, #11), and a
    `stat` buffer read, which goes from a cast at an offset to `a1-&gt;st_size`
    (#12, #13) and takes the field's SIGNED type with it, so the `-1` sentinel
    stops printing as `0xffffffffffffffff` (#14).

    The script restores the default (`opaque`) in its last command: the gate is a
    process environment variable, and the corpus runs in one process, so a stale
    `off` would silently disable the table for every later test in the file.

    Assertions span the combined output of all three passes. `FILE \*` is a
    substring of `_IO_FILE \*`, so every stream assertion pins the text from the
    return type or the `(` onwards.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
